{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_40","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat build/p1c-r3/first.log; ls -la .cache/local/bin; ls -d ~/.local/share/mise/installs/*chez*; ls ~/.local/share/mise/installs/gambit/4.9.8/bin; ls -al build/gambit-ffi/provenance; cat ../datomworld/docs/design/yin.repl-jolt-gambit-ffi-stage2.md | head -100\nrg -n 'sa-baked-global ->' host/gambit/gambitcheck.ss\nsed -n '355,440p' host/gambit/host-vars.ss\n\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_40","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat build/p1c-r3/first.log; ls -la .cache/local/bin; ls -d ~/.local/share/mise/installs/*chez*; ls ~/.local/share/mise/installs/gambit/4.9.8/bin; ls -al build/gambit-ffi/provenance; cat ../datomworld/docs/design/yin.repl-jolt-gambit-ffi-stage2.md | head -100\nrg -n 'sa-baked-global ->' host/gambit/gambitcheck.ss\nsed -n '355,440p' host/gambit/host-vars.ss\n\"","aggregated_output":"gambitcheck: no gambit at /opt/homebrew/opt/gambit-scheme/bin and JOLT_REQUIRE_GAMBIT is set\nmake: *** [gambitcheck] Error 1\ntotal 0\ndrwxr-xr-x@  2 sto  staff   64 Nov 20 00:37 .\ndrwxr-xr-x@ 10 sto  staff  320 Nov 20 00:37 ..\n/Users/sto/.local/share/mise/installs/chezscheme\ngambdoc\ngambuild-C\ngambuild-arm\ngambuild-java\ngambuild-js\ngambuild-php\ngambuild-python\ngambuild-riscv-32\ngambuild-riscv-64\ngambuild-ruby\ngambuild-x86\ngambuild-x86-64\ngambvcs\ngsc\ngsc-script\ngsi\ngsi-script\nscheme-ieee-1178-1990\nscheme-r4rs\nscheme-r5rs\nscheme-srfi-0\nsix\nsix-script\ntotal 8\ndrwxr-xr-x@  3 sto  staff    96 Nov 20 00:14 .\ndrwxr-xr-x@ 17 sto  staff   544 Nov 20 00:45 ..\n-rw-------@  1 sto  staff  1082 Nov 20 00:14 TOOLCHAIN.darwin-arm64.json\n# Jolt Gambit FFI stage 2 — qualified callable modes (DESIGNED, NOT IMPLEMENTED)\n\nStatus: design note only. Compiled 2026-11-19 from the standing rulings; nothing\nhere is implemented on the fork. Implementation authority: owner instruction.\nGoverning artifacts: the astra FFI plan (collab/jolt/1794976827310-architect-\ngambit-ffi-plan.gpt-6-astra.findings.md §2 Callbacks, §3 gate table), the codex\nreview (1795030000000-...findings.md F6), the codex confirmation\n(1795040000000-...findings.md C2/C4/C5), and the P1a-r2 evidence\n(fork gambit-ffi-p1, commits dc6176dc/6507ec1e).\n\n## What stage 1 left refused, and why it is correct to refuse it today\n\n`sa-foreign-callable` / `sa-foreign-callable-collect-safe` (Scheme closures\npassed into C and invoked as C function pointers) raise unsupported on the\nGambit target. The refusal is held by gambitcheck rows and exists because:\n\n- **F6 (codex, P2):** stock Gambit 4.9.8's dynamic closure trampoline generator\n  (`___make_c_closure`) ships machine-code emitters only for 32-bit x86, some\n  PowerPC, and SPARC (lib/os_dyn.c:639-670); without `C_CLOSURE_CODE_SIZE` it\n  returns null (c_intf.c:758-760). There is **no arm64 or x86_64 generator**,\n  so arbitrary runtime closures cannot be converted. Static `c-define`\n  representatives do NOT qualify dynamic closures (the static lookup path is\n  c_intf.c:2914-2923).\n- **P1a evidence:** uncaught callback exceptions kill the process (exit 70);\n  second-Gambit-thread callbacks run only serialized after the foreign call\n  returns; foreign-pthread callbacks survive 3/3 in one shape but are\n  undocumented, and their sibling shape crashes (W3 phase3).\n\n## What stage 2 would add (the design, per astra §2)\n\nQualified modes, in increasing ambition:\n\n1. **Same-thread callbacks** (owner thread; C calls back into Scheme on the\n   thread that entered C is NOT this — this is plain same-thread\n   Scheme->C->Scheme round trips): trampoline + rooting + exception\n   containment. Manual nesting warning applies (txi:19983-19992): no switching\n   between nested Scheme->C->Scheme chains.\n2. **Owner-parked re-entry** (re-entry during a released blocking call): the\n   r2 evidence (serialized foreign entry survives with correct values 3/3,\n   one driving 3 real GC rounds from the foreign pthread) makes this shape\n   empirically plausible, but the codex confirmation C2 explicitly says\n   serialized survival is NOT qualification; this mode needs its own\n   lifecycle evidence before it is even a candidate.\n\nExplicitly refused forever (unless the runtime changes): concurrent re-entry\nwith a live VM (crashes 3/3), generic unrelated-pthread entry (no documented\nattach protocol; the MT experiment's best-effort bridge deadlocks on any\ncollection), any foreign-thread \"mode\" advertised as general.\n\n## Implementation sketch (from astra §2; the design already fixed the shape)\n\n- **Stable C trampoline per registered ABI shape**: generated from the SAME\n  manifest as the call shims (host/gambit/ffi/generated-shims.scm pattern;\n  variadic boundary is part of shape identity — codex's stage-1 fix). The\n  trampoline looks up the rooted closure in an invocation table and calls it.\n  This replaces Gambit's missing machine-code generator entirely; no\n  ___make_c_closure dependency.\n- **Rooting + lifetime**: closure handles rooted at registration; explicit\n  release on teardown; registration table maps the C function pointer to the\n  handle. GC-during-callback must be a tested row, not an assumption\n  (P1a W4 + the real sa-gc-collect from stage 1 make this testable).\n- **Exception containment**: every trampoline catches inside (P1a W4-M5:\n  uncaught exits kill the process; the server.scm pattern is the reference).\n- **Ownership tag**: each registration records its allowed entry mode\n  (same-thread / owner-parked) and the trampoline refuses other modes with\n  message-carrying errors.\n\n## Gates (astra §3's gambitffi-callables, scoped to the qualified modes)\n\n| Row | Evidence |\n|---|---|\n| same-thread round trip | value parity, nesting depth 2-3, callback that allocates |\n| GC during callback | real collection completes while the callback runs; rooted handle survives |\n| exception containment | callback throws; C caller sees the documented error mapping; process survives |\n| lifetime | release then call -> refused message; re-register -> new identity |\n| mode violations | wrong-thread entry refused with the exact message |\n| negative controls | deliberately-wrong rooting; uncaught exception variant |\n\n## Acceptance bar (unchanged from stage 1)\n\nTarget-identified public jolt execution (not standalone fixtures), macOS arm64\nfirst then the Linux leg, state-derived assertive verdicts, watchdogged, seed\nregeneration reproducible, review by a family independent of the author\n(codex implements -> agy reviews -> same-thread confirmations), within the\nC4 must-not-assert list (no generic foreign-pthread claims, no blocking\nqualification, no Linux claim until the Linux leg passes).\n\n## Prerequisites and open items\n\n- Linux leg for stage 1's tier must land first (platform qualification is\n  cumulative; stage-2 callables sit on the same bridge).\n- Lane B (target-aware launchers) must land so callables-gate evidence names\n  its target.\n- The invocation-table protocol needs an Architect glance before\n  implementation ONLY if it departs from astra §2 as written above; otherwise\n  this note is the spec and the astra plan remains the governing ruling.\n\n## Estimate\n\nModerate: trampoline table + rooting protocol + the gate family, on top of\n427:  (check \"sa-baked-global -> #f always\" (sa-baked-global 'jolt-baked-version-early) #f)\n(def-var! \"clojure.core\" \"array?\" jolt-array?)\n\n;; ---- queries answer, they do not raise -------------------------------------\n(def-var! \"clojure.core\" \"queue?\" (lambda (x) #f))\n\n;; No tap registry exists, so nothing is listening and tap> reports that.\n;; Registering one, on the other hand, is a request this target cannot honor.\n(def-var! \"clojure.core\" \"tap>\" (lambda (x) #f))\n\n;; The reader consults this map; empty is the truth here, not a missing name.\n;; empty-pmap is a VALUE, not a constructor — calling it applies the map\n(def-var! \"clojure.core\" \"default-data-readers\" empty-pmap)\n\n;; ---- host errors carry a class and a message --------------------------------\n;; Gambit has no arity introspection (see procedure-arity-mask in\n;; prelude-shims.ss), so seq.ss's structural arity pre-check always passes and\n;; Gambit's own runtime raises instead. Those exception objects have no JVM class,\n;; so (class e), (str e), and a catch clause all fell through to the class-model\n;; default and printed \"#object[:object]\" — which is what `(time)` reported\n;; instead of an arity error. Map Gambit's wording onto the class the equivalent\n;; Chez failure carries. The wording is Gambit's own (there is no arg count or\n;; callee name to recover), but the class and the message are real.\n\n(define (gambit-msg-has? m sub)\n  (let ((ml (string-length m)) (sl (string-length sub)))\n    (let loop ((i 0))\n      (cond ((> (+ i sl) ml) #f)\n            ((string=? (substring m i (+ i sl)) sub) #t)\n            (else (loop (+ i 1)))))))\n\n(define (gambit-host-error? x)\n  (and (condition? x) (not (jolt-ex-info-record? x))))\n\n(define (gambit-error-class x)\n  (let ((m (or (condition-message x) \"\")))\n    (cond\n      ((gambit-msg-has? m \"Wrong number of arguments\") \"clojure.lang.ArityException\")\n      ((gambit-msg-has? m \"Unbound variable\")          \"java.lang.IllegalStateException\")\n      ((gambit-msg-has? m \"Division by zero\")          \"java.lang.ArithmeticException\")\n      ((gambit-msg-has? m \"expected\")                  \"java.lang.ClassCastException\")\n      ((gambit-msg-has? m \"Operator is not a PROCEDURE\") \"java.lang.ClassCastException\")\n      (else \"java.lang.RuntimeException\"))))\n\n(define (gambit-collapse-lines s)\n  ;; Gambit's description runs onto further lines (the procedure and the args it\n  ;; got); a message is one line everywhere else in jolt, so fold them together.\n  (let ((n (string-length s)))\n    (call-with-output-string\n      (lambda (o)\n        (let loop ((i 0) (gap #f))\n          (if (>= i n)\n              #t\n              (let ((c (string-ref s i)))\n                (cond ((memv c '(#\\newline #\\return #\\tab)) (loop (+ i 1) #t))\n                      (else (when (and gap (> i 0)) (write-char #\\space o))\n                            (write-char c o)\n                            (loop (+ i 1) #f))))))))))\n\n(define (gambit-error-tostring x)\n  (string-append (gambit-error-class x) \": \"\n                 (gambit-collapse-lines (or (condition-message x) \"host error\"))))\n\n(register-class-arm! gambit-host-error? gambit-error-class)\n(register-str-render! gambit-host-error? gambit-error-tostring)\n(register-pr-arm! gambit-host-error? gambit-error-tostring)\n\n;; ---- host objects that cannot exist here ------------------------------------\n;; The reader/stream shims built on the jhost record live in the java/ tree this\n;; target does not boot, so no value here can BE one. Answering #f makes every\n;; branch guarded by these predicates dead code — which is why the accessors\n;; behind them need no stand-in, since Scheme resolves a global only when it is\n;; actually reached. printing.ss consults jhost? on the way to a writer, which\n;; is what made `(time 1)` die on an unbound name. (The jhost record itself and\n;; the interop registries are host-statics.ss, which loads before the prelude:\n;; its own (import …) forms intern class tokens through class-model.ss.)\n(define (reader-jhost? x) #f)\n;; jhost-seqable-shim? is java/jutil-colls.ss's, which this boot includes.\n;; No array can be built here (every constructor is degraded below), so nothing\n;; is one; the host callables are multimethods, since no promise exists either.\n(def-var! \"jolt.host\" \"array-value?\" (lambda (x) jolt-nil))\n;; concurrency.ss is not in this boot, so there are no agent threads to mark\n(def-var! \"jolt.host\" \"agent-threads-daemon!\" (lambda () jolt-nil))\n(def-var! \"jolt.host\" \"callable-host?\"\n  (lambda (x) (if (jolt-multifn? x) #t jolt-nil)))\n;; jolt.profile's host surface. Chez defines it in source-registry.ss, which\n;; this boot leaves out; here it is the profile tier's degradation, so\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
