{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_56","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '45,156p' ../datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/store.cljc\nsed -n '1520,1545p;1610,1634p;2210,2300p' ../datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc\nrg -n 'jing/|:digest|:store' ../datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc | head -65\nrg -n 'host-registered|:yin.k/profile|host-modules' ../datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc | head -75\"","aggregated_output":"(defn- supported-text\n  \"The supported `--index-store` forms as one comma-separated string:\n   this namespace's own, then every registered scheme's label in order.\"\n  [extra-schemes]\n  (str/join \", \"\n            (into [\"mem\" (str file-scheme \"<dir>\")]\n                  (map :label)\n                  extra-schemes)))\n\n\n(defn parse-arg\n  \"The `--index-store` value as a spec: `:mem`, or `{:type :file :dir dir}`\n   for `file:<dir>`.  A missing value, an unknown scheme, and an empty\n   directory are refused here — with the supported forms named — so the\n   arguments parser can refuse a bad flag before anything composes.\n\n   `extra-schemes` registers the schemes a storage composition adds, each\n   `{:prefix \\\"<scheme>:\\\" :label \\\"<as documented>\\\" :parse (fn [dir]\n   spec)}`.  They are plain data: this namespace matches a prefix and\n   calls `:parse` with the directory, and never learns what the answer\n   means.  A composition's own checked spec then validates it\n   (`checked-spec` refuses a type it does not know, so a registered\n   scheme whose spec reaches the engine unchecked is still refused).\"\n  ([value] (parse-arg value nil))\n  ([value extra-schemes]\n   (let [supported (supported-text extra-schemes)\n         extra (when (string? value)\n                 (some (fn [{:keys [prefix parse]}]\n                         (when (str/starts-with? value prefix)\n                           (let [dir (subs value (count prefix))]\n                             (if (str/blank? dir)\n                               (throw (ex-info (str \"--index-store \" prefix\n                                                    \"<dir> needs a directory\")\n                                               {:value value\n                                                :supported supported}))\n                               (parse dir)))))\n                       extra-schemes))]\n     (cond\n       (nil? value)\n       (throw (ex-info (str \"--index-store needs a value: \" supported)\n                       {:value value\n                        :supported supported}))\n\n       (some? extra)\n       extra\n\n       (= value \"mem\")\n       :mem\n\n       (str/starts-with? value file-scheme)\n       (let [dir (subs value (count file-scheme))]\n         (if (str/blank? dir)\n           (throw (ex-info \"--index-store file:<dir> needs a directory\"\n                           {:value value\n                            :supported supported}))\n           {:type :file :dir dir}))\n\n       :else\n       (throw (ex-info (str \"Unknown --index-store \" (pr-str value)\n                            \"; supported: \" supported)\n                       {:value value\n                        :supported supported}))))))\n\n\n(defn checked-spec\n  \"Validate an `:index-store-spec` as `yin.repl/create-state` receives it:\n   nil means the `mem` default, `:mem` names it, and a file store is the\n   map `{:type :file :dir dir}` with a non-empty directory string.\n   Anything else is refused with the supported forms named.\n\n   A spec of a type this namespace does not know is refused here rather\n   than opened: a storage composition checks its own spec, and reaches\n   the engine as a prepared `:index-store` handle, so `create-state`\n   never resolves a scheme past the two above\n   (docs/design/yin.repl.dht-composition.md sections 2 and 6).\"\n  [spec]\n  (cond\n    (nil? spec) :mem\n    (= :mem spec) :mem\n    (and (map? spec)\n         (= :file (:type spec))\n         (string? (:dir spec))\n         (not (str/blank? (:dir spec))))\n    spec\n\n    :else\n    (throw (ex-info (str \"Unknown :index-store-spec \" (pr-str spec)\n                         \"; supported: :mem, {:type :file :dir <dir>}. \"\n                         \"A store of any other type is opened by its own \"\n                         \"composition, which injects the prepared handle \"\n                         \"as :index-store\")\n                    {:spec spec\n                     :supported [:mem {:type :file :dir \"<dir>\"}]}))))\n\n\n(defn open\n  \"Open the store `spec` names and keep it plain: `:mem` (or nil) is a\n   fresh in-memory `dao.jing` store; `{:type :file :dir dir}` is the\n   durable directory store at `dir` (dao.space.store/open): exclusive,\n   validating, its HEAD writer, recovery and lock on the handle.  A spec\n   this build cannot open is refused with its reason.\n\n   The answer is a generic `dao.jing` content handle: whatever a\n   composition composes over a store of its own crosses to the engine as\n   a prepared `:index-store`, never through here.\"\n  [spec]\n  (let [checked (checked-spec spec)]\n    (case (if (map? checked) (:type checked) checked)\n      :mem (jing.mem/create-content-mem)\n      :file (durable/open (:dir checked)))))\n\n\n\n\n(defn- run-index-stage\n  \"Drive the code indexer's observer over `program-out`: it commits each\n   forwarded packet as one transaction and publishes the covered indexes.\n   It runs before the evaluator, so a program is indexed whether its VM\n   returns, parks, or raises; the two observers meet only at the medium.\n\n   The publication the round emitted is serviced and settled here, before\n   the round reports (`serve-publication`), so a durable round's frontier\n   is promoted — or its refusal recorded, and rendered by\n   `yin.repl.index/notice` — in the round that published it.\"\n  [state]\n  (-> state\n      (update :indexer index/step (:round state))\n      (serve-publication)))\n\n\n(defn- run-ast-index-stage\n  \"Drive the AST indexer's observer over `program-out`: it adds each\n   forwarded packet's rows and occurrences to the session's `$ast` and\n   `$occ` relations.  It runs before the evaluator for the same reason the\n   code indexer does, and neither indexer reads the other.\"\n  [state]\n  (update state :ast-indexer ast-index/step))\n\n        nil))))\n\n\n(defn- index-round-succeeded?\n  \"True when the index round from indexer `before` to `after` actually\n   committed and published: no recorded failure, not lost, a readable\n   manifest covering every committed transaction, and at least one\n   new transaction. A failed commit or publication preserves the\n   prior manifest (`yin.repl.index`), so it proves nothing.\"\n  [before after]\n  (and (map? before) (map? after)\n       (nil? (:failure after))\n       (not (:lost? after))\n       (some? (:manifest-address after))\n       (= (:published after) (:transactions after))\n       (> (:transactions after) (:transactions before))))\n\n\n(defn- record-call-site-admissions\n  \"Pair each table entry the expander retained since `tables-before`\n   with the indexed program this round committed under the same root,\n   recording `{metadata-entity full-admission-origin}` on `state`.\n\n   Pairing is from COMMIT EVIDENCE, never from searching the published\n   index: `eval-program` captures the round's\n                     (carry-link-identity\n                       (:vm state)\n                       {:id-counter (::link-counter d)\n                        :origins (::link-origins d)})\n                     (:vm state))\n                   (:query-pair expanded))]\n        (consume-failed-round (carry-served\n                                (cond-> (assoc expanded :vm base)\n                                  ;; r3+r4 F4: handles completed\n                                  ;; before the throw ride the recovered\n                                  ;; session: @latest is the latest known\n                                  ;; state of this evaluation, never only\n                                  ;; the pre-evaluator `expanded`\n                                  (seq (:call-provenance-handles @latest))\n                                  (update :call-provenance-handles\n                                          merge\n                                          (:call-provenance-handles @latest))\n                                  ;; r8 F1: failures the stages recorded\n                                  ;; before the throw ride recovery the\n                                  ;; same way, so the diagnostic -- and\n                                  ;; its round Warning -- survives exactly\n                                  ;; once\n                                  (seq (:call-provenance-failures @latest))\n                                  (assoc :call-provenance-failures\n                                         (:call-provenance-failures @latest))\n                                  (seq (:call-provenance-round-failures\n                                        @latest))\n                                  (assoc :call-provenance-round-failures\n                                         (:call-provenance-round-failures\n                                           @latest)))\n                                d)\n                              error))))))\n\n\n(defn- eval-program\n  \"Evaluate one frontend program — the map AST the compilers emit or a\n   datom-literal program — through the §10.1 composition.  The encoder\n   projects it to an input batch of canonical rows with its harvest and\n   declaration rows; the shell appends that batch to `program-in`, drives\n   the expander, drains its summary, and drives the code and AST indexers and then\n   the evaluator only when a program was forwarded.  An expansion failure is reported as data: its\n   batch is consumed, the store keeps its previous macros, and the next\n   input evaluates normally.  An encoding failure throws before anything\n   is appended.\n\n   The observers recover across a gap on their own, but the shell reads the\n   gap counts around the round: an increase on any medium means one or more\n   batches were never run, so resuming as though execution were complete\n   would report a result built on programs the VM never saw.  The loss is\n   reported and the shell refuses further evaluation until `(reset)`.\n\n   A completed program's lexical environment does not outlive it: the VM's\n   environment is restored to the one the round began with, as `vm/eval`\n   does.  A round that throws is consumed by `consume-failed-round`.\"\n  [state program]\n  (if (:ingress-loss? state)\n    [state (str \"Error: \" ingress-loss-text)]\n    (let [state' (update (inject-last-value state) :round inc)\n          tables-before (call-site-tables state')\n          batch (encoder/program-batch program)\n          append (stream/append! (:program-stream state') batch)]\n      (if-not (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome append))\n        [state (str \"Error: program batch not ingested: \"\n                    (name (:dao.stream/outcome append)))]\n        (let [expanded (try\n                         (run-expander-stage state')\n                         (catch #?(:cljd Object\n                                   :clj Exception\n                                   :cljs js/Error) error\n                           {:failed (consume-failed-round state error)}))]\n          (if-let [failed (:failed expanded)]\n            failed\n            (let [[expanded {:keys [errors forwarded]}] expanded\n                  indexed (if (pos? forwarded)\n                            ;; commit evidence is captured BEFORE the\n                            ;; index stage runs: the published programs\n                            ;; and indexer as they stood, so the recorder\n                            ;; pairs only what this round commits\n                            (let [evidence\n                                  {:programs-before\n                                   (indexed-program-roots (:indexer expanded))\n                                   :indexer-before (:indexer expanded)}]\n                              (-> expanded\n                                  run-index-stage\n                                  run-ast-index-stage\n                                  (assoc :call-site-commit-evidence evidence)\n                                  ;; cc3 r2 item 4: this round's\n                                  ;; provenance claims — the roots the\n                                  ;; expander newly retained under\n                                  ;; exactly one admission — ride the\n                                  ;; state into the evaluator stage,\n345:              :pool (jing/observer-state\n419:    (let [{:keys [signal], :as r} (jing/observe-step! content-store pool)]\n426:          (when (identical? missing (jing/get content-store (:address r) missing))\n487:                         :mode :resolved :schema-address (jing/segment-key {})\n543:             :manifest-address (jing/segment-key manifest)})\n132:(defn host-modules\n255:         :module/require (require-handler (host-modules registrations)))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
